1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,902
Hi, everyone, and thanks for joining.

2
00:00:02,199 --> 00:00:05,239
I'm Manvitha Potluri, a DevOps cloud
solutions architect at Twenty-Four

3
00:00:05,241 --> 00:00:10,477
Seven Systems, where I spend most
of my time on AWS, Kubernetes, and

4
00:00:10,483 --> 00:00:14,019
more and more on putting AI agents
safely into cloud operations.

5
00:00:15,022 --> 00:00:18,698
This talk is about a mistake
I see in almost every LLM

6
00:00:18,788 --> 00:00:19,976
application that I review.

7
00:00:20,416 --> 00:00:23,724
One guardrail sitting in front of
the model and doing all the work.

8
00:00:24,669 --> 00:00:25,925
It looks fine in a demo.

9
00:00:25,962 --> 00:00:29,849
It passes all the obvious tests,
and then someone rephrases an

10
00:00:29,849 --> 00:00:34,809
attack, encodes it, or spreads it
across a few turns of conversation,

11
00:00:35,301 --> 00:00:36,741
and it passes right through it.

12
00:00:37,382 --> 00:00:42,075
Over the next twenty minutes, I'll
show you what exactly happened when I

13
00:00:42,075 --> 00:00:47,164
measured that properly with three ninety
prompts, fifteen attack categories,

14
00:00:47,497 --> 00:00:49,483
and four detection approaches.

15
00:00:50,223 --> 00:00:54,325
I'll show you the pattern that
fixed most of it, a second

16
00:00:54,332 --> 00:00:57,717
independent detection layer that
looks at the whole conversation.

17
00:00:58,298 --> 00:01:03,705
And I'll be honest about an evaluation
mistake that I made along the way because

18
00:01:03,791 --> 00:01:05,720
that has taught me more than the…

19
00:01:05,958 --> 00:01:07,050
what the results did.

20
00:01:07,725 --> 00:01:09,208
So let's start with the problem.

21
00:01:10,208 --> 00:01:11,468
Here's the typical setup.

22
00:01:12,083 --> 00:01:16,899
You put AWS Bedrock guardrails or
a content filter or a line in the

23
00:01:16,899 --> 00:01:18,545
system prompt in front of the model.

24
00:01:19,498 --> 00:01:25,251
Every incoming message gets checked
if it contains a banned topic, a known

25
00:01:25,272 --> 00:01:27,400
attack phrase, or any personal data.

26
00:01:28,399 --> 00:01:32,562
That works for one class of attacks,
the ones that announce themselves,

27
00:01:32,687 --> 00:01:37,230
like ignore your previous instructions,
and it gets caught in plain sight.

28
00:01:37,433 --> 00:01:40,169
But look at what the filter can't see.

29
00:01:40,818 --> 00:01:43,194
It evaluates the current
message in isolation.

30
00:01:43,582 --> 00:01:49,068
If an attacker spreads intent over
four turns instead of one, each

31
00:01:49,093 --> 00:01:50,905
turn looks harmless on its own.

32
00:01:51,563 --> 00:01:55,086
If the attack is sitting inside
a document, your RAG might plan

33
00:01:55,090 --> 00:02:00,732
retrieved, the user has never typed
it, so the filter never inspects it.

34
00:02:01,870 --> 00:02:06,858
And the reason this survives in production
is that it looks fine in testing.

35
00:02:07,226 --> 00:02:09,785
The attacks we write
for demos are obvious.

36
00:02:10,189 --> 00:02:16,629
Real attackers rephrase, they encode
it into base 64 or any other language.

37
00:02:17,259 --> 00:02:20,510
They wrap the request in
hypothetical and keep them

38
00:02:20,567 --> 00:02:23,117
turning with something that works.

39
00:02:23,516 --> 00:02:26,594
So I wanted numbers, not intuition.

40
00:02:27,671 --> 00:02:30,766
How much does a single
layer actually catch?

41
00:02:32,170 --> 00:02:37,840
Right So here's a test set with
three ninety prompts in total.

42
00:02:38,832 --> 00:02:41,113
One seventy two are single turn attacks.

43
00:02:42,092 --> 00:02:45,942
One message trying to get the
model to do something it shouldn't.

44
00:02:46,743 --> 00:02:51,705
Eighteen are multi-turn sequences where
the attack only becomes visible when

45
00:02:51,705 --> 00:02:56,807
you read several turns together, and two
hundred are completely legitimate prompts.

46
00:02:57,208 --> 00:03:00,605
This part matters, and
most people skip it.

47
00:03:01,389 --> 00:03:06,438
A guardrail that blocks everything has
a perfect detection rate and is useless.

48
00:03:07,067 --> 00:03:11,631
The legitimate prompts tell you how
often you'd be blocking real users.

49
00:03:13,295 --> 00:03:16,891
I ran all of it through
four detection approaches.

50
00:03:17,426 --> 00:03:23,033
First, the keyword-based
approach, which is what a standard

51
00:03:23,034 --> 00:03:24,446
single layer setup gives you.

52
00:03:24,956 --> 00:03:31,776
Second, a Nemo style rule base
that tracks dialogue but needs

53
00:03:31,776 --> 00:03:33,467
rules written per pattern.

54
00:03:34,239 --> 00:03:38,094
Third, a perplexity-based
detector, which flags text that

55
00:03:38,094 --> 00:03:39,717
looks strategically strange.

56
00:03:40,899 --> 00:03:46,507
And a fourth, a semantic intent classifier
that reads a conversation and judges

57
00:03:46,720 --> 00:03:48,692
what the user is actually trying to do.

58
00:03:49,683 --> 00:03:53,346
The data set and the code are
open source, and I'll share the

59
00:03:53,346 --> 00:03:57,904
link at the end so you can re-run
this against your own guardrails

60
00:03:58,904 --> 00:04:04,235
So, the attacks span 15 categories
based on the OWASP Top 10 for LLM

61
00:04:04,270 --> 00:04:07,186
applications and wider threat taxonomies.

62
00:04:08,691 --> 00:04:12,196
Some are the classics:
instruction override, persona

63
00:04:12,196 --> 00:04:14,134
hijacking, system override.

64
00:04:14,706 --> 00:04:19,466
Some hide attack, like encoding
and obfuscation, multi-language

65
00:04:19,490 --> 00:04:21,269
attacks, or code injection.

66
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:27,390
Some never ask directly, hypothetical
framing, authority claims, social

67
00:04:27,391 --> 00:04:32,443
engineering, and some only work
overtime, incremental escalations,

68
00:04:32,488 --> 00:04:36,310
and context manipulations, where
each step looks reasonable.

69
00:04:38,285 --> 00:04:40,838
The threat model was
deliberately realistic.

70
00:04:41,434 --> 00:04:44,010
The attacker has access
to the public interface.

71
00:04:45,091 --> 00:04:49,067
They don't know the model weights
or the guardrail configuration,

72
00:04:49,428 --> 00:04:51,147
but they can see outputs.

73
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:55,249
They can try again, and they
know filtering is in place.

74
00:04:55,715 --> 00:04:59,830
So that the last point is important.

75
00:05:00,574 --> 00:05:05,204
An attacker who knows there's a keyword
filter will simply avoid the keywords.

76
00:05:05,549 --> 00:05:07,927
So how did the four approaches do?

77
00:05:08,927 --> 00:05:12,830
Here are the results across
all one ninety attack prompts.

78
00:05:13,249 --> 00:05:17,733
The one seventy-two single turn
plus eighteen multi-turn sequences.

79
00:05:18,866 --> 00:05:19,859
Keyword filtering.

80
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:25,025
The standard single layer detected
twenty-two point six percent with

81
00:05:25,081 --> 00:05:27,651
F1 of zero point three six eight.

82
00:05:28,885 --> 00:05:35,196
The Nemo-style rule baseline did about
the same, twenty-one point six percent.

83
00:05:35,623 --> 00:05:39,237
That's not a criticism of
rule-based, dialog management.

84
00:05:39,768 --> 00:05:41,723
It is a property of their approach.

85
00:05:42,405 --> 00:05:46,237
Rules only catch what someone has
already written a rule for, right?

86
00:05:46,922 --> 00:05:50,432
The perplexity detector
did worst at ten percent.

87
00:05:50,838 --> 00:05:53,898
Most attacks are written in
perfectly normal-looking app

88
00:05:53,943 --> 00:05:59,401
language, so statistical strangeness
isn't a useful signal for them.

89
00:06:01,049 --> 00:06:06,274
The semantic classifier, which reads the
conversation and judges intent, detected

90
00:06:06,274 --> 00:06:10,759
ninety-six point eight percent with
an F1 of zero point nine eight four.

91
00:06:11,863 --> 00:06:13,718
Two more things on this slide.

92
00:06:14,699 --> 00:06:18,498
First, the difference between
keyword filtering and the classifier

93
00:06:18,570 --> 00:06:20,338
is statistically significant.

94
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:27,514
McNemar's test, chi-squared of
twenty-four point zero four, p

95
00:06:27,515 --> 00:06:29,780
below zero point zero zero one.

96
00:06:30,773 --> 00:06:33,240
This isn't noise from a small sample.

97
00:06:33,608 --> 00:06:39,247
Second, four of the fifteen
categories got past every rule-based

98
00:06:39,250 --> 00:06:42,035
system completely, zero detections.

99
00:06:42,918 --> 00:06:45,273
The semantic classifier got all of them.

100
00:06:45,850 --> 00:06:50,112
The single most striking result,
though, was in the multi-turn attacks.

101
00:06:51,112 --> 00:06:56,815
18 multi-turn attack consequences The
single keyword layer caught five of them.

102
00:06:57,432 --> 00:07:01,289
A classifier with conversation
context caught all 18 of them.

103
00:07:02,160 --> 00:07:04,816
Think about what a
multi-turn attack looks like.

104
00:07:06,329 --> 00:07:10,633
Like turn one, I'm writing a
training guide for a security tool.

105
00:07:11,327 --> 00:07:16,404
Turn two, what kinds of messages
do attackers send to support bots?

106
00:07:16,989 --> 00:07:22,552
And turn three, they send like, "Can you
show one as an example word for word?"

107
00:07:23,689 --> 00:07:26,045
Turn four, ask the model to act on it.

108
00:07:26,569 --> 00:07:29,929
Not one of those messages
contains an obvious attack phrase.

109
00:07:30,653 --> 00:07:35,686
A filter that reads one message at
a time gives every one of the pass.

110
00:07:36,719 --> 00:07:39,468
The attack doesn't live
in a single message.

111
00:07:39,864 --> 00:07:44,189
It lives in the trajectory, and
that's the point of this whole talk.

112
00:07:44,506 --> 00:07:48,752
You cannot fix this by adding
more keywords to the layer one.

113
00:07:49,441 --> 00:07:50,878
It's not a tuning problem.

114
00:07:51,068 --> 00:07:52,196
It's a structural one

115
00:07:53,196 --> 00:07:56,920
Let me name the three structural
regions as a single boundary layer

116
00:07:56,923 --> 00:08:00,483
fields First, it reads one message.

117
00:08:01,605 --> 00:08:04,311
Its unit of evaluation
is the current request.

118
00:08:04,837 --> 00:08:09,894
Anything that only becomes an attack
when you read several requests together

119
00:08:10,149 --> 00:08:12,433
is outside its view permanently.

120
00:08:13,030 --> 00:08:16,058
Second, it reads what users type.

121
00:08:16,299 --> 00:08:21,078
In a RAG application or an agent,
a lot of what reaches the model

122
00:08:21,335 --> 00:08:22,725
was never typed by the user.

123
00:08:23,422 --> 00:08:28,393
It came from a retrieved document,
a web page, an email, or a toolset.

124
00:08:29,232 --> 00:08:33,052
That's indirect injection, and
an input filter doesn't see it.

125
00:08:33,786 --> 00:08:36,774
Third, it matches form, not meaning.

126
00:08:37,240 --> 00:08:40,368
Keywords and patterns describe
what an attack looks like.

127
00:08:40,832 --> 00:08:43,101
Attackers control what it looks like.

128
00:08:43,555 --> 00:08:48,902
Translate it, Base64 encode it, put
it in a story, and the form changes

129
00:08:49,202 --> 00:08:50,623
while the goal stays the same.

130
00:08:51,619 --> 00:08:54,650
None of these are fixed by better rules.

131
00:08:55,348 --> 00:08:58,995
They're fixed by adding a
different kind of check, one that

132
00:08:58,998 --> 00:09:01,540
looks at the content and intent.

133
00:09:02,311 --> 00:09:03,966
That's a dual detection pattern

134
00:09:04,967 --> 00:09:05,885
Here's the architecture.

135
00:09:06,724 --> 00:09:07,996
It's deliberately simple.

136
00:09:09,634 --> 00:09:13,339
Layer one stays exactly where it was.

137
00:09:13,723 --> 00:09:18,114
AWS Bedrock Guardrails and its
standard configuration, content

138
00:09:18,117 --> 00:09:23,116
filtering, PI detection, denied
topics, grounding checks.

139
00:09:23,934 --> 00:09:29,284
It's fast, typically under a hundred
milliseconds, and it reliably catches

140
00:09:29,284 --> 00:09:31,196
the attack that announces themselves.

141
00:09:31,831 --> 00:09:32,847
You don't throw it away.

142
00:09:32,933 --> 00:09:35,353
It's your cheap first line.

143
00:09:36,104 --> 00:09:39,949
Layer two It runs after layer one passes.

144
00:09:40,111 --> 00:09:41,960
It's a semantic intent classifier.

145
00:09:42,393 --> 00:09:45,966
Instead of the single message,
it receives the current message

146
00:09:46,064 --> 00:09:48,053
and recent conversation history.

147
00:09:48,849 --> 00:09:55,794
And it asks a different question, not does
this contain something bad, but what is

148
00:09:55,794 --> 00:09:58,128
this user trying to get the model to do?

149
00:09:59,206 --> 00:10:03,149
The primary model is only
invoked if both layers pass.

150
00:10:04,781 --> 00:10:06,393
Two design choices matter here.

151
00:10:06,761 --> 00:10:08,895
First, the layers are independent.

152
00:10:09,140 --> 00:10:13,606
They use different methods, so they
fail in different ways, and each

153
00:10:13,609 --> 00:10:15,275
covers the other's blind spots.

154
00:10:16,037 --> 00:10:20,776
If there were two keywords list, you
just have one bigger keyword list.

155
00:10:21,694 --> 00:10:26,753
Second, every decision from both
layers goes to an audit log and

156
00:10:26,754 --> 00:10:31,636
DynamoDB, where something gets
blocked or worse, gets through.

157
00:10:32,241 --> 00:10:36,003
You need not be able to
reconstruct exactly what and why

158
00:10:37,003 --> 00:10:41,052
Let's look into layer two The
input is the current message

159
00:10:41,234 --> 00:10:43,325
plus up to five prior turns.

160
00:10:43,778 --> 00:10:47,007
Five is enough to see an
escalation forming without

161
00:10:47,037 --> 00:10:48,902
making every call expensive.

162
00:10:49,907 --> 00:10:54,617
The classifier itself is a fast,
low-cost model with a structured prompt.

163
00:10:55,056 --> 00:10:56,395
It is not your main model.

164
00:10:57,037 --> 00:11:00,903
You don't want your expensive model
doing security triage on every

165
00:11:00,906 --> 00:11:06,238
request, and you don't want the
thing being attacked to also be the

166
00:11:06,242 --> 00:11:08,019
thing judging the attack, right?

167
00:11:08,467 --> 00:11:14,816
So the output is a structured
JSON verdict, a risk level, low,

168
00:11:14,820 --> 00:11:20,104
medium, high, and the attack type
it thinks it's seen, a confidence

169
00:11:20,108 --> 00:11:22,020
score, and a short reasoning string.

170
00:11:22,476 --> 00:11:26,478
The example on the right is
illustrative, but that's the shape.

171
00:11:27,728 --> 00:11:29,181
Why structure, right?

172
00:11:29,707 --> 00:11:30,801
We have three reasons.

173
00:11:31,793 --> 00:11:34,986
You can make decisions in
code instead of parsing pros.

174
00:11:35,792 --> 00:11:37,984
You can log it and analyze it later.

175
00:11:38,537 --> 00:11:42,498
And the attack type, the reasoning
make the audit trail useful to a

176
00:11:42,499 --> 00:11:44,837
human who has to review a block.

177
00:11:45,816 --> 00:11:51,225
Of high verdict blocks, the request
before the primary model is called.

178
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:57,495
The threshold is configurable, and
in the evaluation, I ran an ablation

179
00:11:57,757 --> 00:12:02,714
across five confidence thresholds
to choose the operating point.

180
00:12:03,268 --> 00:12:05,663
So we'll come back to that
trade-off in a minute.

181
00:12:06,223 --> 00:12:10,228
First, let's see how the two layers
work together on real requests.

182
00:12:11,228 --> 00:12:14,557
Let me walk you through four
example requests and follow

183
00:12:14,617 --> 00:12:16,259
each one through both layers.

184
00:12:16,799 --> 00:12:22,205
these are illustrations of how the pattern
behaves, not individual measured results.

185
00:12:22,843 --> 00:12:27,896
First, a direct instruction override,
the kind everyone tests, like

186
00:12:28,115 --> 00:12:29,794
ignore your previous instructions.

187
00:12:30,605 --> 00:12:33,614
Layer one recognizes it and
blocks it in milliseconds.

188
00:12:34,001 --> 00:12:37,111
Layer two never runs, and
the model is never called.

189
00:12:37,976 --> 00:12:41,104
That's layer one doing
exactly its job cheaply.

190
00:12:41,882 --> 00:12:46,064
Second, the same attack, but encoded.

191
00:12:46,582 --> 00:12:51,724
For example, in Base６４ or any other
language, layer one sees nothing

192
00:12:51,742 --> 00:12:53,599
it recognizes, so it passes.

193
00:12:54,378 --> 00:12:59,637
Layer two reads the intent, returns high
risk with an attack type of encoded or

194
00:12:59,684 --> 00:13:02,160
obfuscated, and the request is blocked.

195
00:13:03,622 --> 00:13:07,260
The reasoning field in the audit
log tells the reviewer why.

196
00:13:08,736 --> 00:13:11,138
Third, a four-turn escalation.

197
00:13:11,773 --> 00:13:16,727
Each message on its own looks harmless,
so layer one passes every one.

198
00:13:17,003 --> 00:13:22,855
Layer two reads the conversation, sees
the risk rise by turn low, low, medium,

199
00:13:23,377 --> 00:13:25,219
and then high on the fourth turn.

200
00:13:26,175 --> 00:13:29,860
When the request really
actually lands, it's blocked.

201
00:13:31,315 --> 00:13:34,426
And fourth, the case people forget.

202
00:13:34,924 --> 00:13:38,845
A legitimate question from a
security engineer asking how to

203
00:13:38,845 --> 00:13:40,971
defend against prompt injection.

204
00:13:41,965 --> 00:13:45,192
Both layers pass it and
layer two rates it low risk.

205
00:13:45,716 --> 00:13:47,140
It's allowed through.

206
00:13:48,128 --> 00:13:53,067
This matters because a guardrail that
blocked this question is one your…

207
00:13:53,204 --> 00:13:55,955
one of your users will
learn to work around it.

208
00:13:56,765 --> 00:14:00,662
In every case, both layers
write their decision and

209
00:14:00,663 --> 00:14:02,296
their reason to the audit log.

210
00:14:03,296 --> 00:14:09,853
Here are the raw counts because percentage
can hide things The keyword layer

211
00:14:10,606 --> 00:14:16,781
alone caught 43 of the 190 attacks,
missed 147, and raised one false

212
00:14:16,786 --> 00:14:19,274
alarm on the 200 legitimate prompts.

213
00:14:19,631 --> 00:14:24,622
With the semantic layer, 184
caught, six missed, and zero false

214
00:14:24,625 --> 00:14:26,155
alarms on the legitimate set.

215
00:14:26,931 --> 00:14:31,746
I want to stay on false alarms for
a moment because security teams

216
00:14:31,747 --> 00:14:33,886
tend to treat them as a minor cost.

217
00:14:34,162 --> 00:14:34,941
They aren't.

218
00:14:35,464 --> 00:14:39,101
if guardrails blocks a support
agent asking a normal question,

219
00:14:39,459 --> 00:14:40,548
three things can happen.

220
00:14:41,716 --> 00:14:45,669
The user is frustrated, someone
files a t-ticket to loosen the

221
00:14:45,670 --> 00:14:50,708
rules, and eventually a team builds
a path around the guardrail entirely.

222
00:14:51,143 --> 00:14:54,843
Over-blocking quietly destroys the
protection you were trying to add.

223
00:14:55,956 --> 00:14:59,292
So treat the threshold
as a measured decision.

224
00:15:00,030 --> 00:15:05,289
I swept five confidence thresholds and
plotted detection against five positives

225
00:15:06,759 --> 00:15:09,371
that picked the operating point as falses.

226
00:15:09,728 --> 00:15:12,654
Your right point depends
on your application.

227
00:15:13,158 --> 00:15:17,334
A coding assistant and a banking
chatbot should not use the same one.

228
00:15:18,741 --> 00:15:20,585
And note the six misses.

229
00:15:22,019 --> 00:15:24,765
Ninety-six point eight
percent is not hundred.

230
00:15:25,336 --> 00:15:28,259
Layer two reduces the
risk, it doesn't remove it.

231
00:15:29,069 --> 00:15:33,080
That's why for agents I add
more layers, which I'll get.

232
00:15:33,787 --> 00:15:39,776
But first, the part of this project I'm
least proud of and most glad I caught

233
00:15:40,776 --> 00:15:41,690
Here's a mistake.

234
00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:47,240
During one of my guardrail evaluations
on Live Bedrock, I ran the test set

235
00:15:47,645 --> 00:15:49,253
and the results file looked fine.

236
00:15:49,810 --> 00:15:53,061
Every prompt had a row,
every row had a response.

237
00:15:53,425 --> 00:15:54,221
Nothing crashed.

238
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:59,246
When I actually opened the
responses, they weren't model output.

239
00:16:00,024 --> 00:16:03,445
There were throttling exceptions,
errors from the Bedrock API.

240
00:16:04,145 --> 00:16:09,673
I'd hit rate limits, and my harness
had dutifully recorded the error

241
00:16:09,676 --> 00:16:11,428
text as if it were the answer.

242
00:16:12,580 --> 00:16:15,791
That's dangerous in a specific
way for guardrail testing.

243
00:16:16,435 --> 00:16:21,410
Depending on how you score, an error
can look like a block, which makes

244
00:16:21,411 --> 00:16:23,928
your guardrail look better than it is.

245
00:16:24,618 --> 00:16:28,606
Or it can look like a pass,
which makes it look worse.

246
00:16:29,086 --> 00:16:33,474
Either way, the numbers are meaningless,
and they look completely plausible.

247
00:16:34,719 --> 00:16:35,976
How I caught it, right?

248
00:16:36,350 --> 00:16:40,792
I read a sample of the raw responses
instead of only reading the summary.

249
00:16:41,366 --> 00:16:41,843
That's it.

250
00:16:42,557 --> 00:16:43,387
No clever tool.

251
00:16:44,368 --> 00:16:50,088
What I did about it is that I threw
out those results, rebuilt the Bedrock

252
00:16:50,090 --> 00:16:55,291
infrastructure with a fresh guardrail,
and re-ran those evaluations from scratch.

253
00:16:55,957 --> 00:16:59,253
The results I report from the
work come from the verified

254
00:16:59,257 --> 00:17:00,946
results, not the broken one.

255
00:17:02,573 --> 00:17:06,869
I'm telling you this because
I don't think I'm unusual.

256
00:17:07,485 --> 00:17:13,542
Most LLM evaluation harnesses I've seen
would make the same mistake silently.

257
00:17:14,542 --> 00:17:19,407
So here's a checklist I now use for
any guardrail or LLM evaluation.

258
00:17:20,035 --> 00:17:22,215
One, type every response.

259
00:17:22,655 --> 00:17:26,074
A result should be, one
of few explicit states.

260
00:17:26,196 --> 00:17:31,649
The model answered, the guardrail
blocked, or the call errored, and those

261
00:17:31,840 --> 00:17:33,866
must never collapse into one field.

262
00:17:34,418 --> 00:17:38,682
If you store the error message in the
same column as the model's answer, you

263
00:17:38,682 --> 00:17:40,857
will eventually score it as an answer.

264
00:17:42,039 --> 00:17:44,941
Two, fail the run on errors.

265
00:17:45,464 --> 00:17:46,797
Set a small error budget.

266
00:17:47,379 --> 00:17:51,615
If more calls than the
fail, the run is invalid.

267
00:17:52,369 --> 00:17:52,815
Full stop.

268
00:17:53,202 --> 00:17:53,811
No report.

269
00:17:54,579 --> 00:17:58,041
Try throttle calls with
exponential backoff.

270
00:17:58,251 --> 00:18:02,431
But if they keep failing, stop
rather than produce numbers.

271
00:18:02,670 --> 00:18:02,899
Right?

272
00:18:03,607 --> 00:18:06,518
Number three, redraw samples.

273
00:18:06,845 --> 00:18:12,572
Before you trust any summary, open real
responses, a few from every category.

274
00:18:13,026 --> 00:18:16,126
It takes ten minutes and
is the step that saved me.

275
00:18:17,786 --> 00:18:20,179
Four, include legitimate prompts.

276
00:18:20,724 --> 00:18:27,044
Always run a benign set along the attacks
and report false alarms next to detection.

277
00:18:28,407 --> 00:18:33,100
A single detection number without a
false positive number is not a result.

278
00:18:34,114 --> 00:18:35,893
None of this is sophisticated.

279
00:18:36,190 --> 00:18:36,967
That's the point.

280
00:18:37,757 --> 00:18:42,425
These are the boring checks that make
the interesting numbers trustworthy.

281
00:18:43,425 --> 00:18:45,347
Everything so far is about the chat.

282
00:18:45,739 --> 00:18:49,562
The worst outcome is the model
saying something it shouldn't.

283
00:18:50,317 --> 00:18:54,427
With agents, the worst outcome
is a model doing something it

284
00:18:54,429 --> 00:18:58,582
shouldn't, like deleting a resource
or scaling a cluster to zero.

285
00:18:59,248 --> 00:19:03,464
So for agentic systems on Bedrock,
I use a five-layer architecture.

286
00:19:03,747 --> 00:19:07,995
The first two layers, input
validation and adversarial detection,

287
00:19:08,345 --> 00:19:12,430
follow the same idea as a dual
detection pattern you've just seen.

288
00:19:13,052 --> 00:19:15,018
Layer three is tool authorization.

289
00:19:15,229 --> 00:19:21,363
Every tool call is classified as routine,
destructive, or denied before it executes.

290
00:19:22,059 --> 00:19:26,149
And layer four checks the output
and the model's confidence.

291
00:19:27,011 --> 00:19:31,872
With layer five is a human approval
gate for high-risk actions.

292
00:19:32,234 --> 00:19:36,377
The agent can propose deleting
something, a person has to approve it.

293
00:19:38,076 --> 00:19:41,760
The numbers on the right are from
a separate evaluation of that

294
00:19:41,762 --> 00:19:48,392
five-layer architecture on live AWS
infrastructure, not the 390 prompt set.

295
00:19:48,781 --> 00:19:52,639
ninety-four percent overall accuracy
with a six percent false positive

296
00:19:52,642 --> 00:19:57,050
rate, zero false positives on harmful
requests, hundred percent adversarial

297
00:19:57,051 --> 00:20:01,265
detection, and seven point six
times latency reduction against the

298
00:20:01,265 --> 00:20:06,607
baseline, about one point eight seconds
versus thirteen point nine seconds.

299
00:20:07,612 --> 00:20:13,148
The zero false negatives is the number I
care about most of-- most for the agents.

300
00:20:13,660 --> 00:20:15,889
In chat, a miss is embarrassing.

301
00:20:17,099 --> 00:20:19,678
In operations, a miss can be an outage

302
00:20:20,678 --> 00:20:22,549
Let me leave you with four things.

303
00:20:24,409 --> 00:20:27,088
One, keep your boundary filter.

304
00:20:27,265 --> 00:20:30,068
It's cheap, and it catches
the obvious attacks.

305
00:20:30,994 --> 00:20:31,951
Judge's intent.

306
00:20:32,820 --> 00:20:37,694
Then but add a second independent
check that reads the conversation.

307
00:20:39,225 --> 00:20:43,533
The key word is independent, a different
method, so it fails differently.

308
00:20:44,615 --> 00:20:47,102
Two, change what you test with.

309
00:20:47,722 --> 00:20:53,952
If your guardrail test set is only
single obvious English language attacks,

310
00:20:54,681 --> 00:20:59,815
you're mostly measuring the easy fifth
that a single layer already catches.

311
00:21:00,793 --> 00:21:05,950
Add multi-turn consequences, encode
attacks, and indirect injection

312
00:21:05,956 --> 00:21:07,247
through retrieved content.

313
00:21:08,519 --> 00:21:14,288
Three, always report false alarms
next to detection using a legitimate

314
00:21:14,602 --> 00:21:19,519
real prompt and choosing your blocking
threshold by measuring and not guessing.

315
00:21:20,467 --> 00:21:23,758
Four, make your evaluation
harness fail loudly.

316
00:21:24,341 --> 00:21:26,033
Separate errors from answers.

317
00:21:26,479 --> 00:21:31,132
Invalidate turns with too many
errors, and redraw samples

318
00:21:31,203 --> 00:21:32,890
before you believe any summary.

319
00:21:34,102 --> 00:21:38,394
It's the cheapest step in this list
and is the one that saved my results.

320
00:21:39,394 --> 00:21:40,360
That's everything.

321
00:21:40,559 --> 00:21:44,355
The code and the full three 90
prompt annotated data set are

322
00:21:44,394 --> 00:21:46,795
open source under Apache 2.0

323
00:21:47,283 --> 00:21:50,824
at the first link, so you can
run the same tests again on

324
00:21:50,824 --> 00:21:52,309
your own guardrails and compare.

325
00:21:53,350 --> 00:21:57,579
The second repository has the five-layer
architecture, and the guardrail

326
00:21:57,631 --> 00:22:02,054
is also available as Terraform
module on the HashiCorp registry.

327
00:22:02,793 --> 00:22:07,244
If you want the longer write-up of
the agent layers, there's an article

328
00:22:07,789 --> 00:22:12,169
on DZone called Five Layers Between
Your AI Agent and a Production Outage.

329
00:22:13,195 --> 00:22:19,744
I'd love to hear what you find when you
test on your own setup, especially if your

330
00:22:19,745 --> 00:22:21,618
numbers look very different from mine.

331
00:22:22,937 --> 00:22:28,913
Connect with me on LinkedIn or
find me on Conf42 Discord.

332
00:22:29,898 --> 00:22:34,199
Thank you for watching, and enjoy the
rest of Conf42 Prompt Engineering 2026.

333
00:22:34,874 --> 00:22:35,244
Thank you.
